14:T82f,<p>The classic relaunch accident goes like this: the new website goes live, everyone celebrates the design — and four weeks later organic traffic has collapsed by 60 percent. Google can’t find the old pages anymore and doesn’t know the new ones yet. The insidious part: the damage only shows with a delay, when the project budget is long spent.</p>
<h2 id="before-the-relaunch-take-inventory">Before the relaunch: take inventory</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Capture all indexed URLs</strong> — via Google Search Console and a crawling tool. You can only redirect what you know about.</li>
<li><strong>Identify your top pages:</strong> which URLs bring traffic, rankings, backlinks? These pages are your capital and deserve special care.</li>
<li><strong>Document existing rankings</strong> so you can compare after the relaunch instead of guessing.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="during-the-relaunch-the-non-negotiables">During the relaunch: the non-negotiables</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>301 redirects for every old URL</strong> to its new counterpart — individually, not wholesale to the homepage. The blanket redirect is the most common and most expensive mistake.</li>
<li><strong>Carry over title tags and meta descriptions</strong> or improve them deliberately — don’t let the CMS regenerate them.</li>
<li><strong>Check internal links:</strong> no links to old URLs, no redirect chains.</li>
<li><strong>Resubmit your sitemap.xml</strong> and watch Search Console for crawl errors.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="after-the-relaunch-two-weeks-on-watch">After the relaunch: two weeks on watch</h2>
<p>The first 14 days decide everything. Check Search Console daily for 404 errors and indexing issues. Individual ranking fluctuations are normal and settle down; wholesale ranking losses on important pages are an alarm signal — and still fixable now, while Google hasn’t forgotten the old signals.</p>
<p>A properly planned relaunch loses no rankings. In most of our projects, visibility actually increases — because the new website is faster, better structured and technically cleaner than the old one.</p>10:["$","main",null,{"id":"main","children":[["$","article",null,{"children":[["$","header",null,{"className":"mx-auto max-w-7xl px-5 md:px-8","style":{"paddingTop":"clamp(7.5rem, 12vw, 10rem)"},"children":[[["$","nav",null,{"aria-label":"You are here","className":"text-[0.82rem]","children":["$","ol",null,{"className":"flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-2 gap-y-1 text-mute","children":[["$","li","0-Home",{"className":"flex items-center gap-2","children":[false,["$","a",null,{"href":"/en","className":"link-slide tap-24","children":"Home"}]]}],["$","li","1-Blog",{"className":"flex items-center gap-2","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":"true","children":"/"}],["$","a",null,{"href":"/en/blog","className":"link-slide tap-24","children":"Blog"}]]}],["$","li","2-Website relaunch without SEO losses: the checklist",{"className":"flex items-center gap-2","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":"true","children":"/"}],["$","span",null,{"aria-current":"page","className":"text-ink font-semibold","children":"Website relaunch without SEO losses: the checklist"}]]}]]}]}],["$","script",null,{"type":"application/ld+json","dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"{\"@context\":\"https://schema.org\",\"@type\":\"BreadcrumbList\",\"itemListElement\":[{\"@type\":\"ListItem\",\"position\":1,\"name\":\"Home\",\"item\":\"https://surhay.design/en\"},{\"@type\":\"ListItem\",\"position\":2,\"name\":\"Blog\",\"item\":\"https://surhay.design/en/blog\"},{\"@type\":\"ListItem\",\"position\":3,\"name\":\"Website relaunch without SEO losses: the checklist\"}]}"}}]],["$","div",null,{"className":"mt-8 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-mute","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"rounded-full bg-accent-600 px-2.5 py-1 text-white","childre